1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,802
<font color="#000000">Machine learning models often look excellent on
benchmark datasets,</font>

2
00:00:04,882 --> 00:00:08,203
<font color="#000000">then break the moment they hit real production data.</font>

3
00:00:08,283 --> 00:00:13,565
<font color="#000000">This paper by Matthew Groh at the MIT Media Lab
argues that the usual explanation,</font>

4
00:00:13,645 --> 00:00:16,116
<font color="#000000">distribution shift, is the wrong frame.</font>

5
00:00:16,196 --> 00:00:21,479
<font color="#000000">Distribution shift only measures that two datasets
differ; it never tells you why.</font>

6
00:00:21,559 --> 00:00:23,441
<font color="#000000">Groh introduces context shift,</font>

7
00:00:23,521 --> 00:00:28,084
<font color="#000000">the semantically meaningful changes in how data are
created, collected,</font>

8
00:00:28,164 --> 00:00:31,158
<font color="#000000">and curated that actually drive those failures,</font>

9
00:00:31,238 --> 00:00:37,437
<font color="#000000">and lays out three concrete ways to address it:
leaning on human intuition and expert knowledge,</font>

10
00:00:37,517 --> 00:00:42,080
<font color="#000000">building dynamic benchmarks, and clearly stating a
model's limitations.</font>

11
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:47,010
<font color="#000000">Benchmark datasets play two roles: they let
researchers compare methods,</font>

12
00:00:47,090 --> 00:00:50,741
<font color="#000000">and they stand in as an imperfect model of the real world.</font>

13
00:00:50,821 --> 00:00:56,014
<font color="#000000">The trouble is that a single static benchmark can
never fully capture the dynamic,</font>

14
00:00:56,094 --> 00:00:58,715
<font color="#000000">high-dimensional complexity of real tasks.</font>

15
00:00:58,795 --> 00:01:04,375
<font color="#000000">So models that appear to match or beat human-level
accuracy on a benchmark routinely err</font>

16
00:01:04,455 --> 00:01:08,684
<font color="#000000">on out-of-distribution and adversarially perturbed
production data.</font>

17
00:01:08,764 --> 00:01:11,963
<font color="#000000">The field describes this gap as distribution shift,</font>

18
00:01:12,043 --> 00:01:17,366
<font color="#000000">with subcategories like covariate shift, prior
probability shift, and concept shift.</font>

19
00:01:17,446 --> 00:01:23,218
<font color="#000000">But these definitions are over-specified for
comparing two data samples and under-specified</font>

20
00:01:23,298 --> 00:01:28,170
<font color="#000000">for evaluating the data-generating process that
actually drives the mismatch.</font>

21
00:01:28,550 --> 00:01:32,164
<font color="#000000">Prior work treats robustness as a difference
between two distributions while disregarding</font>

22
00:01:32,244 --> 00:01:35,858
<font color="#000000">the reasons behind that difference.</font>

23
00:01:35,938 --> 00:01:39,552
<font color="#000000">But the difference is merely a symptom; the disease
is in how data are created,</font>

24
00:01:39,632 --> 00:01:43,246
<font color="#000000">collected, and curated.</font>

25
00:01:43,326 --> 00:01:46,940
<font color="#000000">As Figure 1 shows,</font>

26
00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:50,634
<font color="#000000">interest in distribution shift terminology has
grown enormously over the last decade,</font>

27
00:01:50,714 --> 00:01:54,328
<font color="#000000">with covariate shift papers alone climbing toward
nine thousand per year by 2021.</font>

28
00:01:54,408 --> 00:01:58,022
<font color="#000000">Despite this volume,</font>

29
00:01:58,102 --> 00:02:01,716
<font color="#000000">the vocabulary stays tangled and rarely points
practitioners toward the upstream,</font>

30
00:02:01,796 --> 00:02:05,410
<font color="#000000">semantically meaningful factors they can actually
intervene on.</font>

31
00:02:05,790 --> 00:02:09,859
<font color="#000000">This is a perspective paper that reframes the
robustness problem.</font>

32
00:02:09,939 --> 00:02:13,498
<font color="#000000">Its central contribution is the concept of context shift,</font>

33
00:02:13,578 --> 00:02:18,860
<font color="#000000">which describes the semantically meaningful
upstream factors, sample selection bias,</font>

34
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:21,861
<font color="#000000">adversarial perturbation, and non-stationarity,</font>

35
00:02:21,941 --> 00:02:24,797
<font color="#000000">that drive the distribution shifts we observe.</font>

36
00:02:24,877 --> 00:02:26,265
<font color="#000000">On top of that framing,</font>

37
00:02:26,345 --> 00:02:31,628
<font color="#000000">Groh identifies three practical methods for closing
the benchmark-to-production gap,</font>

38
00:02:31,708 --> 00:02:38,139
<font color="#000000">and illustrates all of them through three recurring
case-study domains: facial expression recognition,</font>

39
00:02:38,219 --> 00:02:40,820
<font color="#000000">deepfake detection, and medical diagnosis.</font>

40
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:45,197
<font color="#000000">Context refers to the semantically meaningful
upstream factors that drive a distribution shift.</font>

41
00:02:45,277 --> 00:02:49,274
<font color="#000000">Rather than fixating on the difference between two
distributions,</font>

42
00:02:49,354 --> 00:02:53,351
<font color="#000000">Groh asks researchers to reason about the three
processes that generate that difference: sample selection bias,</font>

43
00:02:53,431 --> 00:02:57,428
<font color="#000000">for example a new dataset drawn from a demographic
absent from the old one;</font>

44
00:02:57,508 --> 00:03:01,505
<font color="#000000">adversarial perturbations, imperceptible noise
injections that change model behavior;</font>

45
00:03:01,585 --> 00:03:05,582
<font color="#000000">and non-stationarity, where the world itself moves on,</font>

46
00:03:05,662 --> 00:03:09,659
<font color="#000000">like smartphones after 2018 versus flip phones
before 2010.</font>

47
00:03:09,739 --> 00:03:13,736
<font color="#000000">Three methods follow.</font>

48
00:03:13,816 --> 00:03:17,814
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

49
00:03:17,894 --> 00:03:21,891
<font color="#000000">human intuition and expert knowledge can identify
the semantically meaningful features</font>

50
00:03:21,971 --> 00:03:25,968
<font color="#000000">on which models are likely to fail.</font>

51
00:03:26,048 --> 00:03:30,045
<font color="#000000">Second,</font>

52
00:03:30,125 --> 00:03:34,122
<font color="#000000">dynamic benchmarking replaces a single static test
set with continual evaluation against a well-specified,</font>

53
00:03:34,202 --> 00:03:38,199
<font color="#000000">quality-controlled data-generation process,
producing corroborated accuracy,</font>

54
00:03:38,279 --> 00:03:42,276
<font color="#000000">the distribution of scores across many benchmarks.</font>

55
00:03:42,356 --> 00:03:46,353
<font color="#000000">Third, authors should clarify a model's limitations,</font>

56
00:03:46,433 --> 00:03:50,430
<font color="#000000">explicitly stating the contexts in which
performance is known and unknown.</font>

57
00:03:50,810 --> 00:03:53,363
<font color="#000000">Rather than introducing a new dataset,</font>

58
00:03:53,443 --> 00:03:57,520
<font color="#000000">the paper dissects existing benchmarks across three
domains.</font>

59
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:01,816
<font color="#000000">In facial expression recognition it examines seven
benchmarks,</font>

60
00:04:01,896 --> 00:04:04,449
<font color="#000000">from SFEW and MMI to CK+ and MultiPie.</font>

61
00:04:04,529 --> 00:04:09,576
<font color="#000000">In deepfake detection it centers on the DFDC
dataset, the largest to date,</font>

62
00:04:09,656 --> 00:04:16,020
<font color="#000000">with over one hundred twenty-eight thousand videos
from nine hundred sixty consenting actors.</font>

63
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:23,641
<font color="#000000">In medical diagnosis it looks at store-and-forward
teledermatology and the Diverse Dermatology Images dataset.</font>

64
00:04:23,721 --> 00:04:28,353
<font color="#000000">Throughout, it holds up context-aware benchmarks
like BREEDS, WILDS,</font>

65
00:04:28,433 --> 00:04:35,420
<font color="#000000">and Dynabench as models for capturing the
data-generation process instead of a single static snapshot.</font>

66
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:40,082
<font color="#000000">The headline evidence is how wildly performance
swings with the benchmark.</font>

67
00:04:40,162 --> 00:04:44,443
<font color="#000000">One facial expression model, built on AlexNet,</font>

68
00:04:44,523 --> 00:04:48,805
<font color="#000000">ranges from just 48.6 percent accuracy on SFEW to
94.8 percent on MultiPie,</font>

69
00:04:48,885 --> 00:04:53,167
<font color="#000000">against a random baseline of 14.2 percent,</font>

70
00:04:53,247 --> 00:04:57,528
<font color="#000000">so which benchmark you pick determines the story you tell.</font>

71
00:04:57,608 --> 00:05:01,890
<font color="#000000">The pattern generalizes: when researchers
faithfully recreated the ImageNet and CIFAR-10 test sets,</font>

72
00:05:01,970 --> 00:05:06,252
<font color="#000000">state-of-the-art accuracy fell by three to fifteen
percent.</font>

73
00:05:06,332 --> 00:05:10,613
<font color="#000000">And a leading DFDC deepfake detector assigned only
two and eight percent likelihood to</font>

74
00:05:10,693 --> 00:05:14,975
<font color="#000000">genuine deepfakes of Kim Jong-un and Vladimir Putin,</font>

75
00:05:15,055 --> 00:05:19,337
<font color="#000000">precisely the high-context videos that matter in
the real world.</font>

76
00:05:19,417 --> 00:05:23,698
<font color="#000000">In dermatology,</font>

77
00:05:23,778 --> 00:05:28,060
<font color="#000000">benchmarks that barely include dark skin let
systematic errors hide in plain sight.</font>

78
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:31,045
<font color="#000000">A few numbers anchor the argument.</font>

79
00:05:31,125 --> 00:05:38,389
<font color="#000000">A single facial expression model spans 48.6 to 94.8
percent accuracy across seven benchmarks,</font>

80
00:05:38,469 --> 00:05:41,469
<font color="#000000">next to a 14.2 percent random baseline.</font>

81
00:05:41,549 --> 00:05:50,234
<font color="#000000">Faithfully recreated ImageNet and CIFAR-10 test
sets cut state-of-the-art accuracy by three to fifteen percent.</font>

82
00:05:50,314 --> 00:05:58,605
<font color="#000000">A leading deepfake detector gave only two and eight
percent likelihood to real deepfakes of world leaders.</font>

83
00:05:58,685 --> 00:06:03,027
<font color="#000000">The DFDC benchmark holds 128,154 videos from 960 actors.</font>

84
00:06:03,107 --> 00:06:09,976
<font color="#000000">And one dermatology benchmark included just 2.7
percent of the second-darkest skin type,</font>

85
00:06:10,056 --> 00:06:12,740
<font color="#000000">and a single person of the darkest.</font>

86
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:17,373
<font color="#000000">The lasting message is a shift in perspective: stop
treating the benchmark-to-production</font>

87
00:06:17,453 --> 00:06:21,707
<font color="#000000">gap as a mere statistical distance between two
datasets and start treating it as context</font>

88
00:06:21,787 --> 00:06:26,040
<font color="#000000">shift,</font>

89
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:30,373
<font color="#000000">the semantically meaningful changes in how data are
generated.</font>

90
00:06:30,453 --> 00:06:34,707
<font color="#000000">Address it by pairing machine learning with human
intuition and expertise,</font>

91
00:06:34,787 --> 00:06:39,040
<font color="#000000">by moving from static to dynamic benchmarks that
evaluate the data-generation process,</font>

92
00:06:39,120 --> 00:06:43,373
<font color="#000000">and by clearly clarifying each model's limitations.</font>

93
00:06:43,453 --> 00:06:47,707
<font color="#000000">The simplest and most immediately effective step is
honesty: state plainly the contexts</font>

94
00:06:47,787 --> 00:06:52,040
<font color="#000000">in which a model has been evaluated and the
contexts in which its behavior remains unknown.</font>
